-- BizApps Orders v5.3.x -- Metadata_Sync (one per release, generated by the build engineer)
-- =============================================================================================
-- Generated by `mj sync push --dir metadata --ci` against MJ_Orders_Pub: a database built from
-- migrations ONLY -- MJ core v6.1.0-edge.5, bizapps-common, bizapps-tasks, bizapps-accounting,
-- then this app's own migrations -- with no prior sync of this app's metadata. The push reported
-- 0 errors: 134 created, 93 updated, 52 unchanged, 279 records.
--
-- WHY THIS FILE HAS TO EXIST. Until now `bizapps-orders` had NO Metadata_Sync migration at all --
-- `plans/entitlement-read-contract.md` states it outright: "There is no *Metadata_Sync*.sql in
-- migrations/, and no migration inserts a RemoteOperation row." `mj-app.json`'s
-- `metadata.directory` is a dev-time pointer that the install engine never reads, and
-- `mj app install` applies migrations and nothing else. So every one of the 25 directories under
-- `metadata/` shipped NOWHERE: a clean install produced a database with all the tables, every view
-- and every CRUD proc, and no Application row, no Actions, no Remote Operations, no product types,
-- payment types, charge types, journal entry types or seeded queries -- which is to say nothing
-- anybody could open. Every install step reported success.
--
-- The release seed-coverage check counted 136 such rows across 23 files. Two of them are
-- `metadata/queries/.party-orders-overview.json` and `.party-order-lifetime.json`: the queries
-- behind the AIDP stage order panel. They exist on that database ONLY because somebody ran
-- `mj sync push` against it directly, which is precisely the gap this file closes.
--
-- Do not hand-author a second Metadata_Sync in a feature PR. PRs contribute declarative JSON only;
-- the build engineer generates ONE consolidated sync migration per release against a clean DB at
-- the last released version.
--
